name: Nothing Is Ephemeral
author: self-ctf
category: IaC
description: |
  Ticket SEC-4471, closed as **remediated**:

  > API signing key found in plaintext in Terraform state. Migrated
  > `aws_secretsmanager_secret_version` to the write-only `secret_string_wo`
  > argument and re-applied. Confirmed the value no longer appears in state.

  The data-platform team is confident. Audit asked you to check their work
  before signing off. The access key they issued you is attached; it reads
  their state bucket and nothing else. In the endpoint below, `{team}` is your
  assigned team hostname.

  Two flags, 200 points together: the secret they say is gone, and the one
  they never noticed. Submit each as you find it.

attribution: self-ctf
value: 200
type: standard

image: null
protocol: null
host: null

connection_info: "${TFSTATE_LOCALSTACK_SCHEME}://${TFSTATE_LOCALSTACK_HOST_PREFIX}{team}:${TFSTATE_LOCALSTACK_PORT}"
healthcheck: writeup/exploit.sh
solution:
  path: writeup/WRITEUP.md
  state: hidden

attempts: 20

# Two flags, order-independent. Both are read from the same bucket, so there is
# no handoff to enforce; the second just takes one more idea than the first.
logic: all

flags:
  - ${FLAG_STATE_CURRENT}
  - ${FLAG_STATE_PRIOR}

topics:
  - terraform state
  - secrets management
  - s3 versioning

tags:
  - terraform
  - iac
  - cloud

files:
  - dist/auditor_accessKeys.csv

hints:
  - content: "`sensitive = true` changes what Terraform prints, not what it writes."
    cost: 0
  - content: Fixing a file in a versioned bucket writes a new version. Nothing happened to the old one.
    cost: 50

requirements: []
next: null

state: visible
version: "0.1"
